These modals already carry their own meaning of possibility or ability — adding another word with the same meaning creates a redundant, incorrect sentence.

Examples

(a) ❌ He may possibly attend the party.
✅ He may attend the party.

"May" already means "possibly" — adding "possibly" repeats the same meaning.

(b) ❌ It may likely rain.
✅ It may rain.

"May" already implies "likely" — "likely" is redundant here.

(c) ❌ I can be able to do the work.
✅ I can do the work.

"Can" already means "am able to" — pairing both is redundant.

(d) ❌ Because of heavy rain, the students could not be able to attend the meeting.
✅ Because of heavy rain, the students could not attend the meeting.

Same issue: "could" already means "was/were able to" — "be able to" repeats it.

The Core Pattern
                                                Modal	                      Already means	                       Don't add
                                                may / might                	possibly	                      "possibly," "perhaps"
                                                can	                        be able to	                    "be able to"
                                                could	                      was/were able to	              "be able to"


Quick Rule to Remember

A modal verb should never be paired with another word or phrase that duplicates its own meaning.
If the modal already expresses possibility or ability, don't add a second word doing the same job.
